SurfOS application registry
---------------------------
File: rootfs/apps/README   Date: 11/05/26
---------------------------
(docs/superpowers/plans/2026-11-05-desktop-apps.md, track desk)

Each file <name>.app in this folder (/initrd/apps/ when SurfOS runs) puts a program on the
desktop: in Apps > Programs, on a tile of the desktop, and in Files, which opens the file types
it names in it. The desktop (shell/gui*.c, in the kernel) reads the folder each time it starts.
The file is the program's track's own; this one says what it may hold.

    # Paint: pictures, drawn
    program = wpaint
    title = Paint
    types = bmp png
    open-with = gif jpg jpeg
    apps = yes
    desktop = yes
    icon = ................
    icon = .KKKKKKKKKKKKK..
    ... 16 icon lines in all, the top row first

The format
    Text (UTF-8), one setting a line: "key = value", blanks around the key and the value not
    counted. A line whose first character that is not a blank is # is a comment; blank lines
    mean nothing. Lines end with LF or CR LF. Every key but icon may be given once.

    program     required. The program, by its name in /initrd/bin (wpaint is /initrd/bin/wpaint):
                letters, digits, '.', '-' and '_', 1 to 31 of them, not starting with '.'.
                The desktop starts it with no argument from Apps and its tile, and with a
                file's path as its only argument from Files (one argument, spaces and all).
    title       required. Its name in Apps > Programs, under its tile and in Files' Open with:
                1 to 31 bytes of UTF-8, no control characters. The console's characters show it
                (code page 437: a character it lacks is '?').
    types       the file types a double-click in Files opens in it (and Open with offers it
                for): extensions without their dots, separated by blanks, 1 to 8 letters and
                digits each, at most 8 of them, any case (png and PNG are one).
    open-with   the file types Files' Open with also offers it for, as types; a double-click
                does not open them in it. A viewer names its pictures in types, an editor that
                also opens them names them here, so that a double-click shows a picture.
    apps        yes or no: listed in Apps > Programs. yes when not given.
    desktop     yes or no: a tile on the desktop, after the desktop's own, where the screen has
                room for it (Apps > Programs has it at every size). no when not given.
    icon        a row of its 16 x 16 icon: 16 characters, each a pixel, from the palette below;
                the first icon line is the top row. 16 lines or none (none: the desktop's icon
                for a program's window).

    The palette, fixed (0xRRGGBB):
        .  nothing: what is behind shows        A  paper            0xF4F1E6
        K  ink              0x153D49            P  white paper      0xFFFDF5
        N  navy             0x102E3B            W  white            0xFFFFFF
        T  teal             0x197D83            D  black            0x000000
        F  foam             0xB8E9D9            C  coral            0xEE906D
        M  muted grey-teal  0x6C888C            S  sand             0xD0B056
        L  light grey       0xC9CFC4            Y  light sand       0xE8D08A
        B  sky              0x659ECB            R  red              0xC8473B
        G  green            0x4F9A58
    They are the desktop's own colours (include/sys/guidraw.h) and five more. An icon is drawn
    by its program's track: no other product's artwork.

What the desktop does with them
    It reads the .app files of /initrd/apps/ by their names in byte order, the first 32; any
    other file there (this README) is not looked at. A file it cannot take is left out whole,
    with a line in the kernel log saying which and why ("gui: apps/paint.app line 4: types:
    'image/png' is not an extension"): one longer than 4096 bytes, a line longer than 128 bytes
    or with a NUL byte, a line that is not "key = value", a key it does not know or given twice,
    a value that is not as above, no program or no title, a number of icon lines that is not 0
    or 16, or a program that is not in /initrd/bin. An entry whose program is one the desktop
    has as an application of its own (struct gui_app's program: wclock, wcharmap) shows only
    there: its apps and desktop say nothing, its types still count.

    Apps > Programs lists the entries with apps = yes by their titles; the desktop's tiles,
    after the desktop's own and its applications', those with desktop = yes in the same order,
    as many as the screen has room for. Either starts the program, or, when the desktop started
    it and its window is still there, brings that window to the front.

    Files opens a file whose extension an entry's types names in that entry's program, with the
    file's path as its argument (two entries naming the same type: the first, by file name, and
    the kernel log says so). What no entry names keeps the desktop's own rules: a .bmp in the
    Image Viewer, a file Notepad refuses in Hex, any other in Notepad. File > Open with... lists
    every program whose types or open-with name the file's type, then Notepad and Hex.

Bounds, since the kernel parses these: every length above is checked before anything is kept;
nothing is allocated (the entries are kept in the desktop's own state, 32 of them); a file is
read in one piece of at most 4096 bytes; nothing recurses.
